;;; `:ein inconsistent` — a rule that ends a state, stated over its own premises.
;;;
;;; A rule states what it concludes under `:assert`, facts, or a word to the
;;; engine under `:ein`, which is no fact: no state holds it, no rule matches
;;; it and it stands under no arrow (Q-M1f.399). There is one word,
;;; `inconsistent` (Q-M1f.550): where a rule that states it fires, the
;;; saturation of that state ends and the state is dead.
;;;
;;; ── The puzzle ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two people, and at most one of them is the king. `crown` supposes of each
;;; that they are; `one-king` reads two kings and states the key. Nothing is
;;; derived on the way: the rule's premises are the two commitments
;;; themselves.
;;;
;;;   ∅                        alive: no king, and nothing says there is one
;;;   {(king Ann)}             alive
;;;   {(king Bob)}             alive
;;;   {(king Ann) (king Bob)}  dead: `one-king` fires, and the state ends
;;;
;;; So R is the three states below, and the dead set is the pair: its core is
;;; the two commitments, and it is refuted by `one-king`, the rule that
;;; ended it. No `(false)` is written anywhere: the dead state holds the two
;;; `king` facts and no other fact beyond root's.

(relation person Person :why "{?3} is a person")
(relation king   Person :why "{?3} is the king")

(person Ann)
(person Bob)

(rule crown ()
  :match  (person ?p)
  :assert (open (king ?p))
  :why    "suppose {?p} is the king")

(rule one-king ()
  :match (and (king ?a) (king ?b) (neq ?a ?b))
  :ein   inconsistent
  :why   "{?a} and {?b} are both the king, and there is one king")

(solve
  :expect (region
            (model)
            (model (king Ann))
            (model (king Bob))))
